{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Except for his close relative.</b> The reason for the prohibition of impurity is because of the spirit of impurity that remains clinging to a person's body. Therefore it says ״לְנֶפֶשׁ לֹא יִטַּמָּא״ — 'he shall not defile himself for a soul' — and it does not say ״בְּנֶפֶשׁ״, 'through a soul,' because the soul, from its own side, is pure, and there is no aspect of impurity in it at all. Rather, the soul is the cause of impurity: for all other living creatures that do not possess a rational soul but only a vital spirit alone, their death is nothing but the separation of the four elements, and the Angel of Death has no involvement with them at all; therefore there is no severe impurity in them. But the soul of man does not depart except through the Angel of Death, who extracts it against its will, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Avot 4:29): 'against your will you die.' And he comes from the Side of Impurity and brings impurity upon the body — except for the righteous and the unique ones of the generation, whose death is by a kiss, not through the Angel of Death; in them there is no aspect of impurity whatsoever. Therefore our Sages of blessed memory said (Yerushalmi Berakhot 3:1) that on the day Rabbi [Yehudah HaNasi] died, the priesthood was suspended, for the priests were not defiled by him at all." "And it is possible that everyone who is of the seed of Aaron, holy to HaShem — then his death is by a kiss, and impurity departs from them, and this holiness is drawn from Aaron, holy to HaShem, to all who issue from his thigh, for in all of them there is no aspect of impurity whatsoever. Therefore a priest is permitted to involve himself with his close relatives — his father and his mother, his son and his daughter, and his brother — for all of them are of the seed of Aaron, and a priest's wife is considered as his own body. And even though one who involves himself with them incurs seven-day impurity, nevertheless the impurity is not so great as to stand in opposition to the sanctity of the priest, since the essence of the impurity is somewhat lighter. Nevertheless, he may not defile himself for another priest who is not his close relative, for this matter devolves upon his relatives, and if a priest has no relatives, it is preferable that Israelites involve themselves with him. And if neither of these is available, he is a met mitzvah, and it is obvious that it is permitted to involve oneself with him — even for a priest it is permitted. And a High Priest is forbidden for all relatives, for given the greatness of his sanctity, even light impurity mars it." "<b>Except for his close relative who is near to him.</b> Our Sages of blessed memory said (Yevamot 22b): 'his שְׁאֵר' — this is his wife. And the question is: what is this that it says ״הַקָּרוֹב אֵלָיו״ — 'who is near to him' — in the masculine? It should have said ״הַקְּרוֹבָה אֵלָיו״. And why does it call his wife his שְׁאֵר, and why does it place her before his father and mother? And according to the plain sense, it appears we may explain as concluded in the chapter HaBa Al Yevimto (Yevamot 63a): Rabbi Yosei said to Elijah: In what does a woman help a man? He said to him: A man brings home wheat — can he chew wheat? Flax — can he wear flax? Is she not found enlightening his eyes and setting him on his feet? Accordingly, his wife surely sustains him, and she is his שְׁאֵר — that is, his sustenance, which is near to him, for food is transformed into the body of the one nourished by it. And the verse's meaning is that he may defile himself for the one who feeds him, because the food is near to his very essence, adding moisture to him as a transformation of what is drawn from it. And therefore every wife is deemed as his own body, even though she was not created from his own bones except ״זֹאת הַפַּעַם״ — 'this time' — in the original creation. Nevertheless, since for all generations she enlightens his eyes with her שְׁאֵר and her food, it is fitting that he should defile himself for her. And in order that you not err by saying that he may defile himself for anyone who feeds and sustains him, therefore it expressed the food in the term ״שְׁאֵרוֹ״, which also carries the meaning of 'his wife,' as it says (Leviticus 18:12): ״שְׁאֵר אָבִיךָ הִיא״ — 'she is your father's שְׁאֵר.' And it did not say 'his wife' explicitly, in order to explain the reason why he defiles himself for her." "And by way of allusion, one may derive from here a law — that which our Sages of blessed memory said (Mo'ed Katan 25a): a sage who has died — all are considered his close relatives. And concerning this it says: ״כִּי אִם לִשְׁאֵרוֹ הַקָּרוֹב אֵלָיו״, for ״שְׁאֵרוֹ״ means his sustenance, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Berakhot 17b): 'the entire world is sustained only for the sake of Hanina my son,' and so forth. And our Sages of blessed memory said (Bava Kamma 60a): punishment does not come upon the world except for the sake of the wicked, but it does not begin except with the righteous, as it says (Exodus 22:5): ״כִּי תֵצֵא אֵשׁ וּמָצְאָה קוֹצִים וְנֶאֱכַל גָּדִישׁ״ — 'when fire goes forth and finds thorns, and the stack is consumed' — the stack has already been consumed, that is, the righteous. You see that it calls the righteous גָּדִישׁ, a stack: just as a stack is that from which people eat, so the entire world is sustained for the sake of the righteous. And the matter of 'has already been consumed' is that the Holy One, blessed be He, comes with the tactics of war: for it is the way of warriors first to stop up the springs of the city and to burn their stacks, so that they have nothing from which to sustain themselves — as Rashi explained concerning the plagues of Pharaoh (Exodus 8:17). So too the Holy One, blessed be He, does at a time when Israel do not perform His will, blessed be He: He first removes their shade and spoils their stack, so that they have nothing to eat by his merit — as we explained above in the portion Vayechi (50:10), concerning ״בְּגוֹרֶן הָאטָד״, the matter of thorns and the stack. Therefore it says ״כִּי אִם לִשְׁאֵרוֹ הַקָּרוֹב אֵלָיו״ — an allusion to the sage who sustains him, and all are considered his close relatives. And it placed this before father and mother, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Bava Metzia 33a): the lost object of his father and the lost object of his teacher — his teacher's takes precedence, and so forth. And this includes also a time when the righteous man lost something, or his father and mother; therefore it gave precedence to the lost object of his teacher." "<b>For his mother and for his father.</b> For his mother is not of the seed of Aaron, except insofar as she is as the body of her husband; therefore it placed the mother first, since this is the greater novelty. But regarding the High Priest, of whom it says ״לֹא יִטַּמָּא״, it placed the father first, for this is the greater novelty — that he does not defile himself for his father, who is of the seed of Aaron. And if you wish to explain both of these by the principle of 'not only this but also that,' we may say that it is more readily grasped that he should defile himself for his mother, for she suffered greatly over him in pregnancy, birth, and rearing, which is not so with the father; therefore these two places — both ״יִטַּמָּא״ stated here and ״לֹא יִטַּמָּא״ stated regarding the High Priest — all follow the principle of 'not only this but also that.' And furthermore, one's mother is more certain than one's father, for perhaps his mother committed adultery with another and this is not his father; therefore both of them follow the principle of 'not only this but also that.' And furthermore, the mourning of a woman is harsher than the mourning of a man; therefore it placed the mother first, to tell you that if both of them are lying before him, he is obligated to attend first to his mother. But regarding the High Priest, of whom it says ״לֹא יִטַּמָּא״, it placed the father first by the principle of 'not only this but also that' — and this is easy to understand."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:38:10.217529284Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}